   "text": {
    "L1": "We use kilometres, metres and centimetres to measure length. The right unit depends on the size of the thing or distance.",
    "L2": "Here is a common mistake: joining mixed-unit numbers side by side. Two kilometres and fifty metres is not 250 metres.",
    "L3": "Remember the steps between units: one kilometre is 1,000 metres, and one metre is 100 centimetres.",
    "L4": "When we change to a smaller unit, the number gets bigger. So 3 kilometres becomes 3,000 metres.",
    "L5": "For mixed units, change the big unit first, then add the rest. Two kilometres becomes 2,000 metres, and 2,000 plus 50 is 2,050 metres.",
    "L6": "In the worked example, 4 kilometres becomes 4,000 metres. Add 85 metres to get 4,085 metres; the zero holds the empty hundreds place.",
    "L7": "Choose centimetres for small things, metres for rooms and buildings, and kilometres for distances between places.",
    "L8": "Your turn: which unit would you use for the length of a pencil?"
   },
